  abstract: >-
    Applying a legal provision frequently requires both structured rule-based
    reasoning and sensitivity to open-textured terms developed in case law. The
    actio pauliana of Article 42 of the Dutch Bankruptcy Act
    (Faillissementswet, Fw), which allows an insolvency practitioner to avoid
    transactions that prejudice creditors, requires both. We present a
    neuro-symbolic model of Article 42 Fw. Its symbolic core is written in
    Catala in a literate style that keeps statute and formalisation aligned and
    was developed through pair programming, with open-textured terms
    represented as boolean inputs where neural components couple to the
    symbolic solver. A neural routine structures a case's natural-language
    facts into boolean and date inputs. Neural subroutines resolve the
    open-textured terms through case-based reasoning over Dutch civil
    judgments, retrieved through a pipeline that filters on the requirement
    being resolved and expands along the procedural chain of a case.
    Segmenting and labelling those judgments attains high precision and recall
    against a manually annotated gold standard of 100 judgments. On ten
    hand-annotated cases the neuro-symbolic model handles most criteria
    reasonably but struggles with the knowledge requirement. Retrieval depth
    shows no clear quantitative effect, although retrieved judgments provide
    explicit grounding in prior case law.
  keywords:
    - neuro-symbolic AI
    - case-based reasoning
    - retrieval-augmented generation
    - large language models
    - rules as code
